1
00:00:21,140 --> 00:00:26,660
ANN: When I first became aware of the history of our church,

2
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:36,380
I happened to see the big desk that belonged to Dr. Marshall. That's really fascinating,

3
00:00:36,380 --> 00:00:45,760
because it's such a large number, and it began to stimulate many questions in my mind

4
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:52,820
as to what type of a man this minister was, where he came from, why he had the type of death that he had.

5
00:00:53,760 --> 00:01:11,760
And while I have been very interested in the early beginnings of our church, I've already covered the life of Dr. John McMillan and our first pastor, John Clark, and Reverend Mr. Woods.

6
00:01:11,760 --> 00:01:26,320
The man who has really stimulated my imagination, really gotten me involved in doing research, is our third pastor, Dr. George Marshall.

7
00:01:27,820 --> 00:01:37,060
He has a very long pastorate, almost 40 years, and has covered a very interesting period in our history.

8
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:47,220
He came to Bethel Church in 1831 or 1832, so that he was born in 1803.

9
00:01:48,220 --> 00:01:56,980
So that in his youth he had become aware and was under the influence of the teachings of the very early church,

10
00:01:57,540 --> 00:02:06,380
ministers like John McMillan and Thomas Marquis and Elisha Macurdy who was involved in the revival.

11
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:16,260
So that in his youth he was exposed to these very early fiery evangelistic types of pastors.

12
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:22,620
And then through his own life, lived through two terrible wars — the Mexican War and the Civil War.

13
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:34,400
And through research that I have done, I was able to locate a very famous

14
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:42,080
sermon that Dr. Marshall preached in the early period of the Civil War, in which he

15
00:02:42,080 --> 00:02:47,920
tells of some of the young men who had come from Bethel, or had been students at Bethel Academy,

16
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:52,820
and who were killed in the early battles of the Civil War.

17
00:02:54,040 --> 00:03:02,900
I was impressed with Dr. Marshall in that he had the same desire as John McMillan did, to promote education.

18
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:12,000
And one of the first things that he did as pastor of Bethel was to establish an academy, which was known as Bethel Academy.

19
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:21,860
And it was his desire to find young men that he could prepare for the ministry.

20
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:32,860
And much of the work that he did as a teacher at Bethel Academy was to encourage young men to go through Jefferson College and become ministers.

21
00:03:35,180 --> 00:03:42,640
We're also fortunate that upon the death of Dr. Marshall, the various items that are in

22
00:03:42,640 --> 00:03:48,960
the historical room that I have brought here were passed on to us by the children of Dr. Marshall.

23
00:03:49,560 --> 00:03:54,600
We have furniture. We have their family Bible.

24
00:03:55,250 --> 00:04:12,950
We have the pulpit Bible that was used during the period of 1850 to the end of his pastorate, which is the one that I have here, open to Revelation, the seventh chapter, which was a text used when he preached his very famous Civil War sermon.

25
00:04:13,790 --> 00:04:22,910
In research that I did at W&J, I was able to get a copy of the sermon, and I had it

26
00:04:22,910 --> 00:04:25,050
duplicated so that it will be on file.

27
00:04:25,370 --> 00:04:29,930
This is one of the things that I hope to do with the historical room, is to bring down

28
00:04:29,930 --> 00:04:37,270
some of these important documents and items that have almost completely disappeared, or

29
00:04:37,270 --> 00:04:40,150
only be found in historical files —

30
00:04:40,430 --> 00:04:42,930
bring them back and make them available to you,

31
00:04:42,930 --> 00:04:45,970
so that you can sit down and read this.

32
00:04:46,050 --> 00:04:50,250
It is a great concern to be able to do that,

33
00:04:50,590 --> 00:04:52,950
and to read through excerpts

34
00:04:52,950 --> 00:04:54,450
from that text.

35
00:04:55,690 --> 00:04:59,150
I might just tell you that the last time that we met, and this time, this is nice material

36
00:05:04,070 --> 00:05:06,370
that we can use in the historical room.

37
00:05:07,270 --> 00:05:08,870
Would you like to tell them?

38
00:05:24,370 --> 00:05:31,170
Yes — and one of the things is a small picture of Dr. Marshall, and when we compare

39
00:05:31,170 --> 00:05:38,610
it to the one that I have up here, it is a different picture, and will be a very nice addition to this work.

40
00:05:40,690 --> 00:05:48,650
The other items deal with the [unclear] of Dr. Wycoff, and I'll show those to you up here.

41
00:05:51,610 --> 00:06:10,850
This is the type of thing that I'm hoping will continue, and as you, the congregation of Bethel Church, become aware of the type of thing that we're looking for, that you might be able to help us locate some of these things.

42
00:06:14,010 --> 00:06:21,850
I'd like to give you a very brief resume of Dr. Marshall's life, and then just tell

43
00:06:21,850 --> 00:06:29,510
or show you some of the things that I have found on him. And it helps to reveal not just

44
00:06:29,510 --> 00:06:37,190
George Marshall the pastor, but a man who lived at a very trying time in history, and

45
00:06:37,190 --> 00:06:46,290
whose life as an educator and a minister must have been, at times, completely heart-wrenching.

46
00:06:46,590 --> 00:06:55,130
He performed the baptisms on many of these children who were later involved in the Civil War period.

47
00:06:55,390 --> 00:07:01,330
He was a teacher, and he dealt with these young men who went on and were killed during the Civil War,

48
00:07:01,330 --> 00:07:10,730
and he was a pastor who had to perform the funeral. And so he would keep records of all of

49
00:07:10,730 --> 00:07:18,050
the various events. And one of the finest ways that we can study history is really to study Dr.

50
00:07:18,050 --> 00:07:26,410
Marshall's journal, which is the book you see here. And this is one of the projects that I felt

51
00:07:26,410 --> 00:07:35,430
could be undertaken. This is the first recorded record of Bethel Church, which is [unclear]

52
00:07:35,430 --> 00:07:42,810
for the article. And in it he has written the history as he was able to find out

53
00:07:42,810 --> 00:07:46,370
in the back. Also, all of the—

54
00:07:47,360 --> 00:08:00,660
The total membership of the church, as we found it when we first came to Bethel, and from that time on, the record of all of the connections, all of the births, baptisms, marriages and deaths.

55
00:08:01,480 --> 00:08:16,240
The interesting thing about this, in comparison to the records that we keep today, is that he wrote each thing himself, and gave personal information — especially the deaths, how they died,

56
00:08:16,240 --> 00:08:24,380
and under what circumstances. And it certainly was a heart-rending time, because, of course,

57
00:08:25,080 --> 00:08:34,220
with the lack of medical knowledge that they had, and the lack of knowledge of how to provide

58
00:08:35,100 --> 00:08:41,000
immunizations to children — hence records of a whole family of children dying.

59
00:08:43,740 --> 00:08:49,400
The word dysentery occurs frequently, as we've discussed,

60
00:08:49,860 --> 00:08:56,320
because that was probably combined with many types of diseases, because in those

61
00:08:56,320 --> 00:08:58,500
days, they did not know about pure water.

62
00:08:59,320 --> 00:09:06,240
And as we saw last Sunday with our communion set, they had the common cup, which was passed

63
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:09,700
around at the time when it was taken.

64
00:09:09,700 --> 00:09:17,240
And so all of these things come out in the expressions of Dr. Marshall's journal.

65
00:09:17,700 --> 00:09:26,120
You might be interested to know that I asked Tom Starkey, who then had contact with the Xerox Corporation,

66
00:09:26,760 --> 00:09:32,860
to make a duplicate copy of that journal so that the original will not have to be used.

67
00:09:32,860 --> 00:09:41,240
The reason for that is because the journal is very old and the paper is so dry that it's almost deteriorating.

68
00:09:43,470 --> 00:09:54,290
And so we have made two different copies of all the journals, so that the originals do not now have to be used.

69
00:09:55,470 --> 00:10:00,510
Well, to get back to the life of George Marshall — he was born in Union County.

70
00:10:00,830 --> 00:10:03,330
This one, I think, is in the middle part of the state.

71
00:10:03,570 --> 00:10:12,910
I guess Mr. White, Alvin Dinsmore White, he said that he thought it was in central Pennsylvania.

72
00:10:13,790 --> 00:10:19,970
At any rate, he was born in Union County, December the 23rd, 1806.

73
00:10:20,310 --> 00:10:22,690
He was the oldest of five children.

74
00:10:23,590 --> 00:10:29,710
His parents were, of course, Presbyterian and very devout and pious.

75
00:10:30,130 --> 00:10:33,130
His father died when he was very young.

76
00:10:42,830 --> 00:10:52,990
And so he was greatly influenced by his mother, Nancy Adams, and he was sent to Milton Academy.

77
00:10:53,470 --> 00:10:58,810
And just as John McMillan had excellent teachers as a young boy,

78
00:10:58,810 --> 00:11:04,830
George Marshall was very fortunate that he studied under Reverend David Kirkpatrick,

79
00:11:05,030 --> 00:11:12,730
who was a very outstanding educator and did a great deal to try and prepare young men for the ministry.

80
00:11:13,130 --> 00:11:22,850
And it is said that he studied so hard and learned Hebrew, Greek, Latin — those languages in particular —

81
00:11:22,850 --> 00:11:29,450
so that his health was hampered, and that he suffered ill health through most of his life.

82
00:11:30,190 --> 00:11:35,690
He was the type of boy who just loved books. He read constantly.

83
00:11:36,770 --> 00:11:41,590
And it was said that he was prepared really to enter his senior year

84
00:11:41,590 --> 00:11:47,330
at Jefferson College — he had advanced that much in an 18-month study under Dr. Kirkpatrick.

85
00:11:47,910 --> 00:11:53,090
But, of course, the rules of those days were that you had to begin from the beginning.

86
00:11:54,050 --> 00:11:57,570
And so he moved, and I'm not quite sure why.

87
00:11:57,710 --> 00:12:01,170
I think perhaps he had relatives in Cross Creek.

88
00:12:01,670 --> 00:12:09,690
But anyway, he moved to Cross Creek and continued his studies under a very famous pastor, Dr. John Stockton.

89
00:12:10,070 --> 00:12:15,650
And on the 31st of this month, Mr. Alvin Dinsmore White is going to come back,

90
00:12:15,650 --> 00:12:20,330
and I'm going to ask him to tell you a little bit about this man, John Stockton.

91
00:12:21,110 --> 00:12:27,970
I went out to visit Cross Creek, and we took a picture of Dr. Stockton's home.

92
00:12:28,090 --> 00:12:31,590
And it's very likely that George Marshall lived there.

93
00:12:32,690 --> 00:12:36,310
This was very often the custom, that students would board with the pastor.

94
00:12:36,810 --> 00:12:43,170
And he studied theology under Dr. Stockton.

95
00:12:43,170 --> 00:12:55,570
And at the time that he studied there, he became a teacher at the academy, at Cross Creek Academy, and was very well liked by the boys.

96
00:12:55,890 --> 00:13:08,450
And it was there, I think, that he began this trend, which he carried out through his life, of pastor-teacher — not just a minister, but also a teacher as well,

97
00:13:08,450 --> 00:13:15,250
and always with the idea of trying to inspire young men to enter the ministry.

98
00:13:16,070 --> 00:13:22,590
He was also very active in missionary work and was a part of the Western Missionary Society.

99
00:13:23,770 --> 00:13:31,370
There again, the idea of inspiring young men, preparing them through basic training and theology

100
00:13:31,370 --> 00:13:39,750
to go west and help formulate churches along the frontier as the frontier moves west.

101
00:13:40,190 --> 00:13:46,190
You might be interested to know that also, at this time, he met his love —

102
00:13:47,090 --> 00:13:55,630
a young girl by the name of Mary Lee, who was the daughter of one of the outstanding elders, Hugh Lee, of Cross Creek Church.

103
00:13:56,390 --> 00:14:08,470
And in the work that I've done, it's been so inspiring to me that little bits and pieces come together and become very important.

104
00:14:08,470 --> 00:14:14,910
I didn't know anything about Cross Creek, or hadn't even become involved in that part of the research,

105
00:14:14,910 --> 00:14:20,290
when Alvin Dinsmore White was asked to come and speak to us last fall.

106
00:14:20,610 --> 00:14:25,070
And I went up and talked to him after the program was over.

107
00:14:25,350 --> 00:14:32,770
And meeting him, the world opened up, because this man is a historian who has written the

108
00:14:32,770 --> 00:14:33,990
history of his own church.

109
00:14:33,990 --> 00:14:45,750
And as soon as I said George Marshall, he said, oh yes, his wife was Mary Lee, was the daughter of the ruling elder in Cross Creek Church.

110
00:14:46,010 --> 00:14:50,910
And he immediately began to become interested in what we are doing, and to [unclear] him.

111
00:14:51,910 --> 00:15:01,470
And he has given me a great deal of inspiration and some very interesting material concerning the life of Dr. Marshall.

112
00:15:03,990 --> 00:15:30,770
Then after Dr. Marshall graduated from Jefferson College in 1831, he remained on as a teacher there for a period of time, and it was through the president of Jefferson College that he heard that Bethel was seeking a pastor, and he came to Bethel and preached the first Sabbath of June, 1832,

113
00:15:32,130 --> 00:15:37,150
and was ordained and installed as a pastor.

114
00:15:37,690 --> 00:15:42,010
You might be interested to know that he had six children: James Lee —

115
00:15:42,880 --> 00:16:01,360
and I had no information on James Lee at all until I received this notification through the mail from Mr. White, in which he gave me the name of James Marshall, oldest son of Dr. Marshall.

116
00:16:01,440 --> 00:16:12,080
And it was James Marshall who turned back to the church the big desk, and buried his wife and his dad alongside George Marshall.

117
00:16:14,780 --> 00:16:19,820
James Lee — and notice he uses the name Lee, because that was a very important name.

118
00:16:19,960 --> 00:16:26,560
Mary Lee — daughter of, excuse me, son of General James Lee.

119
00:16:26,660 --> 00:16:32,100
Now this is not Robert E. Lee that we talked about.

120
00:16:32,240 --> 00:16:36,940
This is the Lee who was a famous general during the War of 1812.

121
00:16:36,940 --> 00:16:42,400
And Mr. White has a picture of him in his uniform — very amazing.

122
00:16:43,900 --> 00:16:47,780
Sarah Agnes Cadoux — he married a Cadoux.

123
00:16:48,580 --> 00:16:58,280
Mary Amanda Fife, Anna Margaret, and [unclear] — her dad died as a very young child.

124
00:17:07,920 --> 00:17:14,880
So it's very interesting that we find this very close connection between Cross Creek

125
00:17:14,880 --> 00:17:16,220
and Bethel.

126
00:17:16,620 --> 00:17:22,420
And I'm hoping that this slide will bring some pictures back that we showed last fall

127
00:17:22,420 --> 00:17:31,420
of that area, because if you really would like to see an area that would be similar to what Bethel was like

128
00:17:31,420 --> 00:17:37,520
100 years ago, then you should take a ride out to some of these very old

129
00:17:38,040 --> 00:17:41,660
Presbyterian churches in the area of Hickory.

130
00:17:42,160 --> 00:17:46,200
Cross Creek is about five miles from Hickory.

131
00:17:48,540 --> 00:17:50,880
GUEST: It's sort of west of Hickory.

132
00:17:51,640 --> 00:17:53,500
ANN: Yes, it's out in that area.

133
00:17:54,380 --> 00:17:57,260
And really, what you drive into is a little town.

134
00:17:57,480 --> 00:18:04,040
It's special when the white stones meet the marker where Cross Creek Academy actually stood.

135
00:18:04,040 --> 00:18:09,100
And then turned and pointed across the street on one side and said, that is the

136
00:18:09,100 --> 00:18:14,720
home where John Stockton lived, and then turned in the other direction and pointed, and that

137
00:18:14,720 --> 00:18:17,280
is Cross Creek Church, where he attended.

138
00:18:18,120 --> 00:18:23,580
Now you can certainly find all of this becoming alive from the very beginning.

139
00:18:25,260 --> 00:18:31,660
As I said, one of the first things that Dr. Marshall did was open an academy.

140
00:18:31,660 --> 00:18:37,780
The first building was quite small, a brick one-story building, built very close to the

141
00:18:37,780 --> 00:18:39,040
second meeting house.

142
00:18:39,560 --> 00:18:48,900
And then when the second meeting house was torn down in 1855, the old school building

143
00:18:48,900 --> 00:18:54,560
was also taken down, and a new two-story building was erected.

144
00:18:54,640 --> 00:19:01,200
And we have here in the book an artist's conception of what the academy looked like.

145
00:19:01,200 --> 00:19:07,740
I think you can maybe see this very vaguely, and I'm hoping that I could get someone to do

146
00:19:07,740 --> 00:19:15,620
a copy of this so that it could be used in a display in the historical room.

147
00:19:18,440 --> 00:19:27,540
As I said, he at times acted as teacher, especially in the early years, as we've said, because

148
00:19:27,540 --> 00:19:36,480
because of reasons of health, other teachers were brought in. But the academy has existed over a period of from 1830 to 1860.

149
00:19:36,900 --> 00:19:40,220
About 1860 was when it was finally closed.

150
00:19:41,340 --> 00:19:50,640
And the interesting thing is that the bell from the academy was removed and placed on the top of the third meeting house.

151
00:19:52,020 --> 00:19:57,240
And this picture was taken during the pastorate of Dr. Wycoff, because you see the manse here.

152
00:19:57,440 --> 00:20:01,260
Dr. Marshall did not have a manse. He lived on a farm.

153
00:20:01,960 --> 00:20:08,240
But if I were to take my hand and cover this up, you would get a feeling for what the area looked

154
00:20:08,240 --> 00:20:14,720
like when Dr. Marshall was pastor, without the bell on the top. Because while he was pastor, of

155
00:20:14,720 --> 00:20:21,780
course, the bell was on top of Bethel Academy. Then when this church was torn down in 1910,

156
00:20:21,780 --> 00:20:29,000
and the present main building is the white building where the sanctuary is now, that bell

157
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:37,960
again was put into the bell tower. So the bell that you hear each Sunday is the bell that was used to call

158
00:20:37,960 --> 00:20:48,360
a student to his [unclear]. So I think it's a very nice thing to know that we've been able to use some of these things

159
00:20:48,360 --> 00:20:51,480
for such a long period of time. That's pretty reasonable.

160
00:20:52,160 --> 00:20:58,640
It might be a good idea to mention that's the main road to Washington, D.C., and Williamsburg.

161
00:20:59,040 --> 00:21:09,580
GUEST: Because I had an experience with a guy, a lawyer at Westinghouse, and he traveled past that old [unclear].

162
00:21:10,300 --> 00:21:15,060
Turned 23 in Washington, D.C. He says, Bob, that's your main road,

163
00:21:15,060 --> 00:21:19,720
and going down he dated the girl down to the maintenance department, finally married, and of

164
00:21:19,720 --> 00:21:26,060
course she got her problems that way, but he sent up the main drag since it wasn't on 19.

165
00:21:26,480 --> 00:21:35,440
ANN: That's right. I hope that as I talk — and yes, from his make, that he'll make them — because this

166
00:21:35,440 --> 00:21:40,440
is part of what I'm going to accomplish, which is really to relate the things that we're talking

167
00:21:42,380 --> 00:21:46,800
about. Remember, things that are important to you, that you'll speak up and mention them.

168
00:21:47,140 --> 00:21:50,240
Now, I gave you an assignment last week.

169
00:21:50,460 --> 00:21:57,100
I asked you to look at this picture and tell me where that house is located at this present time.

170
00:21:57,360 --> 00:21:59,460
Now, how many of you know?

171
00:21:59,620 --> 00:22:00,200
Come here.

172
00:22:02,940 --> 00:22:08,280
So, any of you know that one? One, two, three, four. Robbie knows, but I don't.

173
00:22:09,020 --> 00:22:14,180
Well, most of you, I'm sure, thought that this was on Marshall Road.

174
00:22:15,660 --> 00:22:21,120
Because Dr. Marshall bought a tract of land that was quite large.

175
00:22:21,880 --> 00:22:24,600
It was a farm.

176
00:22:25,240 --> 00:22:32,600
And on it he had constructed, with bricks that were made on the premises, a two-story farmhouse.

177
00:22:33,600 --> 00:22:43,000
And this building was added to at the death of Dr. Marshall, and was passed on to his children —

178
00:22:49,820 --> 00:22:51,820
Sarah, [unclear] and James.

179
00:22:52,180 --> 00:22:56,020
Well, James would have been the oldest son in the family.

180
00:22:58,580 --> 00:23:12,900
And you can see from the picture — but it was bought up by Mr. Hicks.

181
00:23:15,700 --> 00:23:18,120
I don't know whether Dan will help me with that.

182
00:23:28,320 --> 00:23:30,880
That was my first [unclear].

183
00:23:38,340 --> 00:23:57,340
Dr. Marshall was the owner of a farm adjoining Bethel Church on the east, containing 60 acres, purchased from William Tidball, April 1, 1835, on which he erected a fine brick residence, now greatly improved and occupied by Mr. E. M. Dixon.

184
00:23:57,340 --> 00:24:14,320
And it goes on to say he also purchased 53½ acres adjoining from Mr. William McCully on March 28, 1861, and a small piece of the foot of the church field from Jacob Warlock on March 28, 1861.

185
00:24:15,280 --> 00:24:18,020
These tracts were developed into a fine farm.

186
00:24:19,320 --> 00:24:30,900
Well, the house, of course, is just down the road — that magnificent house with the beautiful trees and the very wide yard in the front.

187
00:24:39,360 --> 00:24:47,720
And suddenly realized this was the home of Dr. George Marshall, and probably through the

188
00:24:47,720 --> 00:24:54,320
years he added on to it so that it was more than the original building. I think you can see

189
00:24:54,320 --> 00:25:01,400
that there were additions made to it. And we're so very fortunate that the people who bought it

190
00:25:01,400 --> 00:25:08,700
were willing to leave it and restore it and add to it rather than tear it down.

191
00:25:08,700 --> 00:25:19,500
So really, it's some very ironic case that in Bethel's history so much of what it was

192
00:25:19,500 --> 00:25:26,540
was a part of the growth of the church and still remains. Very few churches can point

193
00:25:26,540 --> 00:25:30,180
to a place like the Miller Homestead

194
00:25:33,430 --> 00:26:00,730
and say that is the site of the original log cabin in which Bethel Church began, or point to this beautiful home and say that is where Dr. George Marshall lived during his time as pastor, or take a walk through that cemetery and be able to find the grave of our five pastors who are buried here.

195
00:26:00,730 --> 00:26:07,770
These are all things that are very important to know about and teach our children about.

196
00:26:12,110 --> 00:26:15,310
This is a picture of Jacob Morlock, Sexton.

197
00:26:15,870 --> 00:26:21,090
And in the third meeting house, before the bell was used, they used the handbell.

198
00:26:21,430 --> 00:26:28,410
We have two of these. This is the largest one, I brought down to show you,

199
00:26:28,970 --> 00:26:36,430
and the other one is still up in the historical room. And I wonder if you would like to walk up

200
00:26:36,430 --> 00:26:41,690
for a minute and talk about the historical room. It's not really [unclear] as far as display is

201
00:26:41,690 --> 00:26:48,310
concerned, but it has the furniture and the computer set up. Would you like to take a walk?

202
00:27:05,730 --> 00:27:10,750
GUEST: What year was this? 1950?

203
00:27:10,750 --> 00:27:21,830
Summer of '51. And, uh, the [unclear] — and quite a lot of little bugs —

204
00:27:24,930 --> 00:27:29,970
that's all right — quite a lot of little bugs. And if the day was cold

205
00:27:30,740 --> 00:27:37,460
they stayed in, but if the day was hot, you'd sit up on deck and they'd [get on] every leg of your chair.

206
00:27:38,240 --> 00:27:39,800
They'd get on you for every corner.

207
00:27:41,320 --> 00:27:47,000
So I had my mind made up, and I said goodbye to the captain, but I would say thanks for the buggy ride.

208
00:27:49,620 --> 00:27:56,500
But his Norwegian was all right, but I doubt if he could have gotten that English.

209
00:27:58,680 --> 00:28:04,320
After returning to America, we spent a year in deputation work for the board.

210
00:28:05,140 --> 00:28:11,340
After that we went to Cambridge, Massachusetts, where we served in Chinese student alumni services.

211
00:28:11,340 --> 00:28:19,880
This work was for Chinese students, cut off as they were from their homes. This was a

212
00:28:19,880 --> 00:28:26,440
comfort to the students, and a very happy experience for us. After three years of this

213
00:28:26,440 --> 00:28:34,240
we returned in 1956 to Pittsburgh. Here I served under the presbytery, visiting chiefly people

214
00:28:34,240 --> 00:28:42,380
living in the housing projects in the vicinity of McKees Rocks, and preached at Coverdale. In 1959

215
00:28:42,380 --> 00:28:49,380
I came to Bethel Presbyterian Church as associate pastor — eight years of very

216
00:28:49,380 --> 00:28:58,520
happy service here, and enriched ourselves with many splendid friends. On May 1st, 1967, I retired.

217
00:29:01,060 --> 00:29:08,720
And that's it. I might add that I retired to the regret of many of the members of Bethel Church.

218
00:29:09,800 --> 00:29:11,220
Well, you can add that.

